WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.579
Hi, I'm Linchpin!

00:00:02.579 --> 00:00:07.735
vLLM rewrote Kimi K3's decode step for one user on GB300 GPUs.

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:09.454
Here's how it works.

00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:14.610
For one user, most of a decode step is spent between

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.962
kernels, not reading weights.

00:00:15.962 --> 00:00:19.342
So vLLM fused the communication into the work around it.

00:00:20.883 --> 00:00:25.003
Each layer starts with projections tuned for the batch size, then the attention core.

00:00:25.423 --> 00:00:29.568
The output projection, its all-reduce and the residual are one kernel.

00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:33.336
Partial sums go straight into buffers the other GPUs read.

00:00:33.756 --> 00:00:37.513
The router and the latent projection are split across the GPUs instead

00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:38.765
of repeated on each.

00:00:39.185 --> 00:00:41.688
One persistent kernel runs the routed experts.

00:00:41.688 --> 00:00:45.621
The tail folds the second reduction and the next norm together.

00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.828
While the GPUs talk, a side stream pulls the next weights into cache.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.638
Stock vLLM: one hundred and thirty microseconds and twenty-three kernels per layer.

00:00:57.058 --> 00:01:02.376
The specialized path: sixty-nine microseconds and thirteen kernels, the fused ones outlined.

00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:08.070
On sixteen GPUs, a step fell from seven point three eight to

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.466
three point nine milliseconds.

00:01:09.466 --> 00:01:11.909
Streaming the weights alone takes about one.

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.658
Speculative decoding sits on top.

00:01:16.078 --> 00:01:18.412
A small drafter proposes seven tokens.

00:01:18.832 --> 00:01:21.739
The target checks eight rows in one step, about the cost of

00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:22.708
a batch of eight.

00:01:23.128 --> 00:01:25.875
The headline forces six accepted tokens a step.

00:01:25.875 --> 00:01:28.965
That is where eight hundred and forty-two comes from.

00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:34.011
The fair comparison: same sixteen GPUs, no speculation.

00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:37.639
The other multipliers divide by different GPU counts.

00:01:38.808 --> 00:01:42.143
For one user, the overhead between kernels is the target.

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:44.812
Fuse the communication into the work around it.

00:01:46.525 --> 00:01:50.463
Collectives inside the kernels, a verify that costs a batch of eight,

00:01:50.463 --> 00:01:52.431
and a denominator under every speedup.

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:55.005
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:55.621
I'm Linchpin.

00:01:55.621 --> 00:01:55.929
Bye!
